{"schema":2,"commit":"ca6f5855f708922f23b0d993ed8170b1470ae492","branch":"fix/windows-daemon-startup","base_sha":"90d92cf6b6c3e1575dfecfee3c94b65dcda27ee6","message":"Start the Windows daemon without a console window\n\nThe 15-minute keepalive Scheduled Task launched blamely.exe - a\nconsole-subsystem binary - with an interactive token, so Windows handed it a\nreal console: a command window flashed open and shut four times an hour, and\nonce more at every sign-in. Task Scheduler has no setting that suppresses this\n(its \"Hidden\" checkbox hides the task from the library, not the window), and\nevery wrapper fails the same way: cmd /c start /b, powershell -WindowStyle\nHidden and conhost --headless each show a console of their own, and the\nscheduler cannot launch a .lnk at all. A non-interactive principal does\nsuppress it, but registering one needs administrator rights.\n\nThe one thing Windows never gives a console is a GUI-subsystem image, so the\nautostart entries now run blamelyw.exe: a small binary that takes no arguments\nand starts the blamely.exe beside it with CREATE_NO_WINDOW. It is signed with\nthe same profile as blamely.exe in the release pipeline. Every entry falls back\nto the old command line when the launcher is absent, so a source build or an\narchive from before this change still works - it just keeps flashing.\n\nThree more Windows-only fixes, all found while testing that one:\n\nReport an autostart task that could not be rewritten. A task registered by an\nelevated install refuses schtasks /Create /F from a non-elevated one, and both\nthe installer and the daemon self-heal swallowed that failure, so those\nmachines silently kept running the old command line - every autostart fix we\nship was undeliverable to them, invisibly. Install and doctor now compare what\nis registered against what this build would register, name the difference and\nprint the command that repairs it; the daemon logs it too.\n\nKeep the daemon running across an update. `blamely install` kills the old\ndaemon with taskkill /T, which reached the installer itself through the updater\nprocess tree - so the step that starts the new daemon never ran and the machine\nhad no daemon at all until the keepalive fired, up to 15 minutes later,\ndropping every edit in between. The synchronous kills no longer take child\ntrees (the deferred uninstall cleanup still does, and a test now guards that),\nand the daemon is started through the Scheduled Task, which makes it a child of\nthe scheduler rather than of a tree the update tears down.\n\nRecord every update attempt in ~/.blamely/update.log, one line each, with the\nreason on failure. Auto-updates run unattended, so a machine that quietly\nstopped updating left nothing to diagnose; doctor surfaces the last line.\n\nFinally, blamelyw.exe carries an icon and version/company/description fields,\nand the release build stamps both binaries' version resources from the tag -\nblamely.exe was shipping 1.8.2 while its resource still claimed 1.6.7. An\nanonymous, metadata-less helper that starts another program from a scheduled\ntask is precisely the profile a heuristic scanner treats as malware.","coding_time_nanos":7128849800,"totals":{"added_lines":1460,"ai_added_lines":853,"human_added_lines":607,"deleted_lines":34,"ai_deleted_lines":15,"human_deleted_lines":19,"files":32,"models":{"claude-opus-5":853}},"by_tool":{"claude":{"lines":853,"deleted_lines":15,"accepted_lines":853}},"by_gen_type":{"chat":0,"cli":868,"completion":0,"human":626},"files":[{"path":".github/workflows/release.yml","type":"MODIFIED","added_lines":78,"deleted_lines":4,"ai_added_lines":47,"human_added_lines":31,"ai_deleted_lines":1,"human_deleted_lines":3,"lines":[{"start":112,"end":112,"type":"add","author_type":"AI","tool":"claude","model":"claude-opus-5","gen_type":"cli"},{"start":112,"end":112,"type":"delete","author_type":"AI","tool":"claude","model":"claude-opus-5","gen_type":"cli"},{"start":113,"end":113,"type":"add","author_ty